From: Mikael Hug <mikaelhug@gmail.com>
Date: Sat, 31 Oct 2026 00:46:48 +0000
Subject: [PATCH] media: apple: Add Apple Video Decoder driver

Add a V4L2 stateless decoder driver for AVD, the video decode block of
the Apple M2 (t8112), starting with H.264.

AVD is a Cortex-M3 coprocessor in front of the decode engines. The driver
copies the open avd-fw firmware (https://github.com/AsahiLinux/avd-fw,
MIT, apple/avd-fw-v3-t1.bin) into the coprocessor code memory and boots
it. The firmware only forwards engine interrupts to the mailbox; the
driver builds the instruction stream for each frame from the V4L2
controls and feeds it to the VP FIFO register, then kicks the
post-processor when the VP reports completion.

The instruction encoding, register offsets, buffer sizes and the
compressed reference layout come from the asahi avd driver (asahi tree,
drivers/media/platform/apple/avd, by sofus and Janne Grunau, from traces
of the macOS AppleAVD driver). This is a rewrite of it for t8112 only:

- the firmware config ("AVDc" + codec mask) is read where avd-fw puts
  it, at 0x400, after checking the image size; asahi read it at the end
  and fell back to "all codecs" when it was missing;
- the instruction push is bounded, and every value from userspace that
  indexes an array, sizes a buffer or is parsed by the CPU is checked;
- a stuck or failed job is recovered by a runtime PM power cycle instead
  of a pmgr reset of ps_avd_sys, which also holds the DART and can race
  DART TLB flushes and engine DMA (a test build with a one-jiffy
  watchdog reset the SoC that way);
- the in-flight job has one owner (IRQ, watchdog or recovery), the IRQ is
  disabled while the domain is powered down, and unbind waits for the
  running job;
- output is NV12 only, 8-bit 4:2:0 progressive; 10-bit and 4:2:2, which
  asahi advertised with the wrong pixel formats, are refused.

H.264 is slice based: the engine decodes a whole picture in one job, so
slices are copied into a per-context bitstream buffer and the job is
submitted at the first slice queued without
V4L2_BUF_FLAG_M2M_HOLD_CAPTURE_BUF.
A picture whose last slice keeps the flag is released by the m2m core
undecoded; GStreamer and FFmpeg do not do that.

Tested on a Mac mini (M2) as a module with GStreamer v4l2slh264dec:
bit-exact against FFmpeg for baseline, main, high and 4-slice 320x240,
1080p and 2160p High; v4l2-compliance 57/57, 0 warnings. That was before
the last robustness rework (job ownership, recovery, request validation,
bitstream buffer), which is build-tested only. The recovery path has not
run on hardware.

